\ ff-test: lines
\ With FF_SAFE_MEM the interpreter follows only the return-stack cells
\ it pushed itself. A return, `leave` or a loop's back edge that meets a
\ cell a program pushed with `>r`, or a frame it took apart, raises -25
\ (return stack imbalance). They used to go wherever the cells said:
\ `16 >r 16 >r` in a definition forged its return address, and the host
\ crashed. Runs only in FF_SAFE_MEM builds.

: p 32 emit . ;

\ A return through cells pushed with >r.
: forge 16 >r 16 >r ;
forge
' forge catch p cr
: refill r> r> 2drop 16 >r 16 >r ;
refill

\ A loop whose body took its frame apart, and a leave with a cell on top.
: unframe 5 0 do r> r> r> drop drop drop loop ;
unframe
: leave-late 5 0 do 1 >r leave loop ;
leave-late

\ The frames the interpreter pushed still work: dropping a whole one
\ returns to the caller's caller, and >r ... r> balanced in a loop body
\ is fine.
: skip r> r> 2drop ;
: caller skip 99 p ;
caller 7 p cr
: balanced 3 0 do i >r r> p loop ;
balanced cr
